From a733fa7198ec6526ee7b340c8c0bac040394d988 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Henri Sundelin <henris42@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 21:19:41 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Emscripten: downloads that fall back to the whole file,
 leaks on failed blocks, and a build with clang 19

Found by building the engine with Emscripten 3.1.69 and running it in
Chromium against a test server that can fail block requests and can
ignore Range. With a server that answers Range requests and does not
fail, nothing here changes.

- A server without Range support (or one that does not expose
  Content-Range): the "bytes=0-0" probe gave a null header, which went
  into std::string and aborted the program. The file is now fetched in
  one piece (FetchInBlocks::no_range), as when concurrent download is
  off. A probe that fails takes the same way instead of assuming a file
  of 50000 bytes.
- A block that failed 11 times fell back to the whole file, but the file
  arrived without the extra last byte that block downloads carry and
  that the readers leave out, so a .glb came out as "Invalid glTF
  binary". fetch_2_success adds that byte whenever the readers will
  leave one out. The url is no longer put on the error list before the
  fallback has been tried; fetch_2_error does that when it fails too.
- FetchInBlocks::fetch_failed: a failed block request is closed and its
  user data freed before the retry (both were left behind on each
  failure); after giving up, later failures of the same file do not call
  failed() again; the count of concurrent downloads is given back.
- fetch_success counted a request down twice when it came back empty.
- fetch_block: the copy of the url made for emscripten_fetch is freed
  (emscripten_fetch keeps its own).
- set_string kept a copy of every string passed from JS that was used
  only for its length; set_new_script kept every script it was ever
  given. Both are freed now.
- Equalizer.hh: two calls of the outer class's member functions from a
  nested class without an object, which clang 19 rejects.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
